We can use two pointers, one for each string, to iterate over the characters from the end of str1 and the beginning of str2. The loop continues either until the characters do not match (for both strict and non-strict cases) or until it exhausts one of the strings. The `strict` flag controls the behavior when a mismatch is encountered.

```c
int count_conditional_matches(const char* str1, const char* str2, bool strict) {
    int count = 0;
    int i = strlen(str1) - 1; // Pointer for the end of str1
    int j = 0; // Pointer for the beginning of str2

    while (i >= 0 && j < strlen(str2)) {
        if (str1[i] == str2[j]) {
            count++;
            i--; // Move backward on str1
            j++; // Move forward on str2
        } else {
            if (strict) {
                // Stop counting as soon as a mismatch is found
                break;
            } else {
                // Skip the non-matching character in str1 and continue matching with next character in str2
                i--;
            }
        }
    }

    return count;
}
